<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1494">1494</a>–<a href="/years/1573">1573</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Juan Ginés de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sepulveda">Sepúlveda</a> was a Spanish humanist, philosopher and royal chronicler. Drawing on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aristotle">Aristotle</a>'s idea of natural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavery">slavery</a>, he argued that the Spanish conquest of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> was just, because the indigenous peoples were barbarous and could be subjected for their own good. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valladolid-debate">Valladolid debate</a> of 1550–1551 he defended this view against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolome-de-las-casas">Bartolomé de las Casas</a>, but the crown did not allow his treatise Democrates alter to be printed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/juan-gines-de-sepulveda.jpg" alt="Juan Ginés de Sepúlveda" width="320" height="458" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: José Maea · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Juan_Gin%C3%A9s_de_Sep%C3%BAlveda_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
